# mlir-tblgen-shard.patch
#
# Backports the --op-shard-count / mlir-src-sharder op-sharding feature from
# LLVM 19.1.7 (tag llvmorg-19.1.7) to the LLVM 18.0.0git used by tpu-mlir
# (commit c67e443895d5b922d1ffc282d23ca31f7161d4fb).
#
# Why does op sharding speed up compilation?
#   mlir-tblgen -gen-op-defs generates all op definitions into a single
#   .cpp.inc. A dialect with hundreds of ops (TopOps has ~196) produces one
#   enormous translation unit that takes ~8 minutes to compile — the build
#   bottleneck. Op sharding splits this into N translation units that compile
#   in parallel, reducing wall time to ~4 minutes with 8 shards.
#
#   The obstacle to naive splitting is op registration. The original
#   registration mechanism is addOperations<AddOp, ConvOp, ..., OpN>(),
#   which (Dialect.h) expands to one RegisteredOperationName::insert<OpI>()
#   call per op, ALL in the same translation unit. insert<T> (OperationSupport.h)
#   constructs Model<T>, whose body calls T::getOperationName(),
#   T::getInterfaceMap(), T::parse(), T::verifyInvariants(), TypeID::get<T>(),
#   T::getAttributeNames(), etc. — all static-member accesses that require
#   T's complete definition to be visible (included) in that TU. So
#   addOperations<all ops>() in one TU forces ALL op definitions into that TU.
#
#   This is NOT a universal C++ rule. The standard ([temp.inst] §13.9.2)
#   requires a template argument's complete definition only when the template
#   body uses the type in a completeness-requiring way (member access,
#   inheritance, sizeof, static-member calls). A template that only uses T*
#   or T& can be instantiated with an incomplete (forward-declared) T — the
#   standard's own example shows `Z<char>* p` does not instantiate Z<char>.
#   The constraint here comes specifically from insert<T>'s body calling
#   static members of T, not from template instantiation in general.
#
#   The fix: replace the single addOperations<all ops>() call with per-op
#   RegisteredOperationName::insert<single op>(dialect) calls, scattered
#   across N shard .cpp files. Each insert<T> still requires T's complete
#   definition (same static-member calls), but only for ONE op — so each
#   shard TU only includes its own slice of op definitions. A dispatcher
#   function (registerTopDialectOperations) calls per-shard registration
#   functions (registerTopDialectOperations0..N) via ordinary function calls
#   that require no op type visibility. This decouples compilation units.
#
#   Only op definitions (method bodies, the compilation bottleneck) are
#   sharded into GET_OP_DEFS_N guards. Op declarations (GET_OP_CLASSES: class
#   declarations, forward declarations) are not sharded — they go into the
#   .h.inc included by all shards. Declarations are cheap to compile (no code
#   generation, no template instantiation) and each shard may reference other
#   ops' types, so all declarations must be visible everywhere.
#
#   The verifier function tag (StaticVerifierFunctionEmitter constraintPrefix)
#   is defensive naming from LLVM 19, not a correctness requirement: verifier
#   functions are static (internal linkage), so same-named functions in
#   different .o files don't conflict. The tag makes backtraces more debuggable
#   but is not needed for sharding to work.
#
# Why not use LLVM 19's mlir-tblgen directly?
#   LLVM 18→19 changed MLIR's C++ API (e.g. ::mlir::LogicalResult moved to
#   ::llvm::LogicalResult, mlir::Operation gained getRawDictionaryAttrs(),
#   a new Properties model was introduced). mlir-tblgen generates C++ code
#   that directly references ::mlir:: and ::llvm:: symbols, resolved against
#   the host project's MLIR headers at compile time. Using LLVM 19's tblgen
#   with LLVM 18 headers produces code that won't compile (20+ errors on
#   TopOps.h.inc alone). So the shard logic must be backported into an LLVM 18
#   tblgen that generates 18-compatible code.
#
# How this patch was created:
#   Generated by diffing LLVM 18 c67e4438 originals against the patched
#   sources. The patched sources were derived from LLVM 19.1.7's shard
#   logic, adapted to LLVM 18's API: only shard-related changes were ported,
#   unrelated LLVM 19 API changes (LogicalResult namespace, interleave
#   signature, emitSourceFileHeader signature, Properties model, etc.) were
#   deliberately excluded.
#
# Files modified (5 from LLVM 18 c67e4438, 1 from LLVM 19 llvmorg-19.1.7):
#
#   mlir-tblgen/OpGenHelpers.h
#     Add declaration of shardOpDefinitions() — new in LLVM 19.
#
#   mlir-tblgen/OpGenHelpers.cpp
#     Add --op-shard-count cl::opt and shardOpDefinitions() implementation.
#     Verbatim from LLVM 19. Splits op defs into N roughly-equal shards.
#
#   mlir-tblgen/OpDefinitionsGen.cpp
#     Core change. Splits the monolithic emitOpClasses() into three functions:
#       emitOpClassDecls() — emits GET_OP_CLASSES (op class declarations)
#       emitOpClassDefs()  — emits op class definitions (per-shard with tag)
#       emitOpDefShard()   — emits GET_OP_DEFS_<n> guard + registration hooks
#     emitOpDecls/emitOpDefs now call shardOpDefinitions() and loop over shards.
#     Derived from LLVM 19 but adapted to LLVM 18: emitSourceFileHeader takes
#     2 args (not 3), interleave uses lambda form (not os parameter form).
#
#   include/mlir/TableGen/CodeGenHelpers.h
#     StaticVerifierFunctionEmitter: constructor gains optional StringRef tag
#     (used to unique symbol names across shards). emitOpConstraints split into
#     collectOpConstraints() + emitOpConstraints() (was combined with emitDecl
#     bool). Mirrors LLVM 19 exactly for these two changes.
#
#   lib/TableGen/CodeGenHelpers.cpp
#     Implementation of the header changes above. getUniqueOutputLabel() gains
#     tag parameter. emitOpConstraints() no longer takes emitDecl. Mirrors LLVM 19
#     for these functions only; unrelated LLVM 19 changes (auto→const auto*,
#     ::mlir::LogicalResult→::llvm::LogicalResult, getDiag→emitError) excluded.
#
#   mlir-src-sharder/mlir-src-sharder.cpp
#     This file is extracted from LLVM 19 (the tool doesn't exist in LLVM 18).
#     Single fix: add #include "mlir/Support/LogicalResult.h" — LLVM 18's
#     LLVM.h doesn't transitively include it, but the source uses LogicalResult.
#

